---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Container from "../components/Container.astro";

// Change this date whenever the page changes. Every tool listed below was
// checked against the code (BaseLayout, middleware CSP, the form handlers) and
// the published Google Tag Manager container on the date shown.
const updated = "October 12, 2026";

const h2 = "text-2xl text-ink font-display font-bold tracking-tight mb-3";
const h3 = "text-lg text-ink font-display font-semibold tracking-tight mb-2";
const label = "text-ink font-semibold";
const link =
  "text-forest underline decoration-2 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-ember";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description="What elefoxstudio.com collects, which outside services see it, and how to turn the tracking off."
>
  <section class="pt-24 md:pt-32 pb-10">
    <Container size="default">
      <h1 class="display-xl text-[clamp(2.5rem,6vw,4rem)]">Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="lede mt-6">Last updated: {updated}</p>
    </Container>
  </section>

  <section class="pb-24 md:pb-32">
    <Container size="default">
      <div class="prose-legal max-w-2xl space-y-10 text-graphite leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          elefox studio LLC ("elefox studio," "we," "us," or "our") is a
          Florida limited liability company. This page covers what
          elefoxstudio.com collects, which outside services see it, and how to
          turn the tracking off. We kept it plain on purpose.
        </p>

        <div>
          <h2 class={h2}>The short version</h2>
          <ul class="space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
            <li>
              When you fill out a form, your answers come to our inbox and into
              the client portal we use to keep track of inquiries.
            </li>
            <li>
              This site uses Google Analytics, the Meta Pixel, and Cloudflare
              Web Analytics, so Google, Meta, and Cloudflare get information
              about your visit. Google and Meta use it for their own purposes
              too, and for Meta that includes advertising.
            </li>
            <li>
              We don't sell your information to anyone for money. We do let
              Google and Meta collect visit data, as described below.
            </li>
            <li>
              You can turn the tracking off. <a href="#opt-out" class={link}>Here's how</a>.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class={h2}>What you tell us</h2>
          <p>
            When you fill out a form, book a call, or email us, we get what you
            choose to share: your name, email, phone number, business details,
            and whatever you write. We use it to reply to you and, if we work
            together, to do the work.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4">Here's where a form submission goes:</p>
          <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
            <li>
              Resend, our email delivery service, sends it to our inbox, which
              runs on Google Workspace.
            </li>
            <li>
              A copy goes into our client portal, a system we built and run,
              where we keep track of inquiries. If our spam filter flags a
              submission, it stays in email until we've looked at it.
            </li>
            <li>
              If you fill out one of our discovery questionnaires, we may send
              your answers to an AI service so it can write us a short summary
              before we talk. While you work, the questionnaire saves a
              draft in your own browser so you don't lose your place. It
              clears the draft when you send it, or when you clear your
              browser's site data.
            </li>
            <li>
              We sometimes use AI tools to look up your business or help us
              draft a reply.
            </li>
            <li>
              If you book a call, you'll do that on Calendly's site, and
              <a href="https://calendly.com/legal/privacy-notice" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>Calendly's privacy notice</a>
              covers what you enter there.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="mt-4">
            Please don't send health information about yourself or anyone else
            through our forms or by email. If you're a healthcare provider,
            that includes anything about your patients or clients, so describe
            your practice in general terms. Our forms and inbox aren't set up
            for health information.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tracking" class="scroll-mt-28">
          <h2 class={h2}>Tracking on this site</h2>
          <p>
            Here's every outside tool this site loads as of the date at the
            top, what it collects, why we use it, who gets the data, and how
            to say no.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4">
            Google and Meta can recognize your browser on other sites that use
            their tools, so they can collect information about your activity
            here and elsewhere over time.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4">
            Our cookie notice is a heads-up, not an on/off switch. Tracking
            runs whether or not you click "Got it." To turn it off, use the
            options below.
          </p>

          <div class="mt-8 space-y-8">
            <div>
              <h3 class={h3}>Google Analytics, loaded through Google Tag Manager</h3>
              <p>
                <strong class={label}>What it collects:</strong> the pages you
                view, how you found us (a search, a link, a post), your rough
                location based on your IP address, your device and browser,
                and how long you stay. Google Analytics also records some actions on
                its own: how far you scroll, links you click that lead to
                other sites, files you download, and when you start or send a
                form. We also count clicks to book a call and taps on our
                phone number or email. We don't send your name, your email, or
                anything you type into a form to Google Analytics. It sets
                cookies (their names start with <code>_ga</code>) so it can
                tell a returning browser from a new one.
              </p>
              <p class="mt-3">
                <strong class={label}>Why:</strong> to see which pages actually
                help people and which ones don't.
              </p>
              <p class="mt-3">
                <strong class={label}>Who gets it:</strong> Google. Google Tag
                Manager is the tool that loads Google Analytics onto our pages.
                Google also uses data from sites like ours under its own terms.
                <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>Here's how Google explains it</a>.
              </p>
              <p class="mt-3">
                <strong class={label}>How to opt out:</strong> install
                Google's
                <a href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>Analytics opt-out browser add-on</a>
                or use <a href="#opt-out" class={link}>our switch</a>.
                Blocking cookies in your browser also stops it from
                recognizing you on later visits.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h3 class={h3}>Meta Pixel</h3>
              <p>
                <strong class={label}>What it collects:</strong> that you
                visited, which pages you viewed, the page you came from, your
                IP address, and details about your browser and device. It also
                tells Meta which buttons you click and reads page details like
                titles and descriptions. When
                you send one of our forms, it also tells Meta that a lead came
                in and from which page, but not what you wrote. It sets a
                cookie named <code>_fbp</code>, plus one named <code>_fbc</code>
                if you arrive from a Facebook or Instagram link. If you're
                logged in to
                Facebook or Instagram in the same browser, Meta can connect the
                visit to your account. Meta has a setting called automatic advanced
                matching that sends a scrambled copy of contact details you
                type into a form. We keep it turned off, so the pixel doesn't
                send what you type into our forms. The pixel doesn't load at
                all on our CRM for therapists page (<code>/crm-for-therapists</code>).
              </p>
              <p class="mt-3">
                <strong class={label}>Why:</strong> to see whether people who
                find us on Facebook or Instagram end up visiting the site, and
                so we have the option to show our ads to people who've been
                here.
              </p>
              <p class="mt-3">
                <strong class={label}>Who gets it:</strong> Meta. Meta also
                uses this data under its own
                <a href="https://www.facebook.com/privacy/policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>privacy policy</a>,
                including to choose the ads you see on Facebook and Instagram.
              </p>
              <p class="mt-3">
                <strong class={label}>How to opt out:</strong> change your
                <a href="https://accountscenter.facebook.com/ad_preferences" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>ad preferences in Meta's Accounts Center</a>,
                clear past activity or disconnect future activity on Meta's
                <a href="https://www.facebook.com/off_facebook_activity" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>activity off Meta technologies</a>
                page,
                block third-party cookies or use a tracker blocker, or use
                <a href="#opt-out" class={link}>our switch</a>.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h3 class={h3}>Cloudflare Web Analytics</h3>
              <p>
                <strong class={label}>What it collects:</strong> page views,
                the page you came from, your browser, your country, and how
                fast our pages load. It doesn't set cookies.
              </p>
              <p class="mt-3">
                <strong class={label}>Why:</strong> to get a simple, cookie-free
                count of visits and keep an eye on page speed.
              </p>
              <p class="mt-3">
                <strong class={label}>Who gets it:</strong> Cloudflare
                (<a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>privacy policy</a>).
              </p>
              <p class="mt-3">
                <strong class={label}>How to opt out:</strong> most tracker and
                content blockers stop it. Our switch doesn't cover this one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h3 class={h3}>Cloudflare Turnstile</h3>
              <p>
                <strong class={label}>What it collects:</strong> signals from
                your browser and device that help tell people from bots, plus
                your IP address, which our server also passes to Cloudflare to
                check the result. It
                only loads on pages with a form.
              </p>
              <p class="mt-3">
                <strong class={label}>Why:</strong> so our forms don't drown in
                spam.
              </p>
              <p class="mt-3">
                <strong class={label}>Who gets it:</strong> Cloudflare.
              </p>
              <p class="mt-3">
                <strong class={label}>How to opt out:</strong> it has to run
                for a form to go through. If you'd rather skip it, email us at
                <a href="mailto:hello@elefoxstudio.com" class={link}>hello@elefoxstudio.com</a>
                instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h3 class={h3}>Fonts and code libraries</h3>
              <p>
                <strong class={label}>What it collects:</strong> your IP address
                and basic browser details, the same as any web request. Our
                fonts load from Google Fonts, and the little product demos on
                our CRM pages load an animation library from jsDelivr, so your
                browser fetches those files from them.
              </p>
              <p class="mt-3">
                <strong class={label}>Why:</strong> so the site looks and moves
                the way we designed it.
              </p>
              <p class="mt-3">
                <strong class={label}>Who gets it:</strong> Google and jsDelivr.
              </p>
              <p class="mt-3">
                <strong class={label}>How to opt out:</strong> a content
                blocker can stop these files from loading. The site still
                works. It just won't look quite as nice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h3 class={h3}>Hosting</h3>
              <p>
                Railway hosts this site and our client portal. Like any web
                server, it sees your IP address when you load a page. Our
                server logs can include your IP address and, if a form
                submission gets flagged as spam, the email address on it. We
                also hold IP addresses in memory for an hour or two to stop
                anyone from flooding our forms.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div>
              <h3 class={h3}>Our own cookies and browser storage</h3>
              <p>
                These stay between your browser and our site. None of them go
                to anyone else.
              </p>
              <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
                <li>
                  <code>ef_cache_reset</code>: a cookie that cleared out an old
                  redirect problem. It lasts a year, and the site stops
                  setting it after October 31, 2026.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <code>disc_</code> plus a form name: a cookie that remembers
                  you unlocked a private questionnaire. It lasts 60 days.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <code>elefox_cookie_ok</code>: remembers you closed our
                  cookie notice.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <code>elefox_noga</code>: remembers you turned tracking off
                  with our switch.
                </li>
                <li>
                  Questionnaire drafts: your answers, saved in your browser
                  until you send them or clear your browser's site data.
                </li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <div id="opt-out" class="scroll-mt-28">
              <h3 class={h3}>Our switch: turn tracking off on this site</h3>
              <p>
                Use the
                <a href="/privacy?noga=1#opt-out" class={link}>opt-out link</a>
                once and Google Analytics and the Meta Pixel stop sending visit
                data from that browser. Their script files still load, so
                Google and Meta see a basic request from your browser, like any
                web request. It sticks until you clear your
                browser's site data. Changed your mind?
                <a href="/privacy?noga=0#opt-out" class={link}>Turn tracking back on</a>. It works one browser at a time, and it
                doesn't cover Cloudflare Web Analytics.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class={h2}>Do Not Track and Global Privacy Control</h2>
          <p>
            Some browsers can send a Do Not Track or
            <a href="https://globalprivacycontrol.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>Global Privacy Control</a>
            signal. Right now this site doesn't change anything when it gets
            one, so Google Analytics and the Meta Pixel load as usual. If you want them off, use the
            <a href="#tracking" class={link}>options above</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class={h2}>How we use your information</h2>
          <p>
            We use your information to reply to you, to do the work if we take
            on a project together, to understand how the site is doing, and to
            see whether our posts, and any ads we run, bring people here. We don't sell your information to
            anyone for money. We do let Google and Meta collect visit data
            through the tools above, and they use it for their own purposes
            too. For Meta, that includes advertising.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class={h2}>Who else sees it</h2>
          <p>Only the services on this page, each for its own piece of the job:</p>
          <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
            <li><strong class={label}>Resend</strong> delivers form emails to us.</li>
            <li><strong class={label}>Google</strong> runs our inbox (Google Workspace), Google Analytics, Google Tag Manager, and Google Fonts.</li>
            <li><strong class={label}>Meta</strong> runs the Meta Pixel.</li>
            <li><strong class={label}>Cloudflare</strong> runs Web Analytics and Turnstile.</li>
            <li><strong class={label}>Railway</strong> hosts this site and our client portal.</li>
            <li><strong class={label}>AI services</strong> summarize questionnaires and help us research and draft replies.</li>
            <li><strong class={label}>jsDelivr</strong> serves the animation library in our product demos.</li>
            <li><strong class={label}>Calendly</strong>, if you book a call there.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="mt-4">
            Resend and Railway handle your information to provide their
            service to us. Some AI services we use may also keep and use what
            we send them under their own terms. Google and Meta also use what
            their tools collect under their own privacy policies, linked
            above. We may also share information if the law requires it, and
            if elefox studio LLC is ever sold or merges with another business,
            the information we hold could go with it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class={h2}>How long we keep it</h2>
          <p>
            We keep inquiry emails and portal records for as long as they're
            useful for following up and for our business records. If you ask,
            we'll delete them from our inbox and portal, unless we need to
            keep something for taxes or a contract. Copies in our providers'
            logs and backups clear on their own schedules. Google, Meta,
            Cloudflare, Railway, Resend, and our AI services keep what they
            collect for as long as their own policies say.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class={h2}>Your choices</h2>
          <p>
            You can ask what we have about you, ask us to fix it, or ask us to
            delete it. Just email
            <a href="mailto:hello@elefoxstudio.com" class={link}>hello@elefoxstudio.com</a>.
            We can delete what's on our side. For what Google and Meta
            collected, use their tools linked above. If the law where you live gives you
            more rights, tell us and we'll work with you on it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class={h2}>Security</h2>
          <p>
            We take reasonable steps to protect your information, like
            encrypted connections and hosting providers we trust. Nothing sent
            over the internet is completely secure, so we can't promise
            perfect security.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class={h2}>Children</h2>
          <p>
            Our site is for businesses and isn't directed to children under
            13. We don't knowingly collect information from children.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class={h2}>Changes to this policy</h2>
          <p>
            When we add or remove a tool, or change how we handle your
            information, we'll update this page and the "last updated" date
            at the top.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class={h2}>Contact us</h2>
          <p>
            Questions about this policy or your information? Reach us at:
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3">
            elefox studio LLC<br />
            Coral Springs, FL<br />
            <a href="mailto:hello@elefoxstudio.com" class={link}>hello@elefoxstudio.com</a><br />
            <a href="tel:+19547806508" class={link}>954.780.6508</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
